WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:18.180
We turn now to item five on the agenda, which
is the New Congenital Heart Disease Review.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:25.220
This has been an immensely complex review,
and it builds upon a history.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.930
I am going to invite Ian to introduce the
paper, but I also invite Bruce Keogh to talk

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.580
through the paper, if I may.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.790
Thank you very much.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:42.750
You have a weight of paperwork in front of
you, because this is a very comprehensive

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:49.190
review, as it needs to be to do justice to
the subject matter and to satisfy the terms

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.330
of reference that this Board set two years
ago.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:01:00.570
The way in which the review has been conducted
has, I think, been very inclusive, open and

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:05.400
transparent, and rigorous.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:17.600
We have set out clear models for service delivery,
standards and service specifications, and

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.649
achieved on the basis of the best available
evidence – making clear the basis of that

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:28.030
evidence – a broad consensus.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:35.230
Not unanimity, but a broad consensus on these
models, these standards and these service

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:36.350
specifications.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:46.579
They include: a focus on networks, with three
tiers; standards for surgical caseloads and

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:54.240
team size; eliminating occasional practice;
co-location of adults’ and children’s

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:03.029
sites; better early diagnosis, including foetal
screening, in line with the PHE Committee

00:02:03.029 --> 00:02:14.400
latest advice; support and communication with
patients and families to improve their experience;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.950
better information, and a focus on palliative
care, end-of-life care, and transitions from

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:28.459
childhood to adulthood; as well as peer review
and better arrangements for monitoring and

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:31.390
audit.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:41.150
Our analysis suggests that these proposals
are affordable and deliverable, bearing in

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:47.159
mind existing workforce constraints.

00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:56.890
We are, through discussion with providers,
I think close to achieving solutions that

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:04.290
would enable these models, standards and service
specifications to be implemented across the

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.340
country.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:16.549
That process is ongoing, and we are expecting
providers to come back by the end of October

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:18.650
with their propositions.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:26.810
I want to just put on record NHS England’s
thanks to Chris Hopson and NHS Providers for

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:31.720
their help in facilitating that process.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:40.610
We would very much like to reach a consensus
approach with providers in a timely way, so

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:49.329
that these models, standards and specifications
can be implemented across the NHS from April

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:50.499
of next year.

00:03:50.499 --> 00:03:59.409
However, of course, we are continuing to prepare
for the alternative option of going out to

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:02.680
procurement should that prove necessary.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:12.099
There are, I think, three wider implications
that I would like to touch on.

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:20.200
First of all: these are national standards,
and as we develop approaches to devolving

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.610
power within the Health Service – through
co-commissioning with CCGs, or devolution

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:33.040
arrangements such as arrangements with Manchester
– we have been very clear in those arrangements

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:39.350
that we expect national standards to continue
to apply, and that would of course be the

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.010
case in relation to the standards that we
are discussing today.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:48.640
It is still going to be a National Health
Service, the importance of the ‘N’ in

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.510
the NHS.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:59.790
Secondly, one way of looking at this is as
the first of the series of rolling reviews

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:07.510
of specialist services that we have also promised,
and we will need to reflect on and learn from

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.880
what has been successful in this review as
we initiate that programme.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:20.760
Then thirdly, the approach we are taking both
to the discussions with providers and the

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:27.410
network solutions that are being developed
are heralding, I think, a rather different

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:37.500
emphasis across the NHS on promoting collaboration
between different acute providers.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:44.190
In this they are part of a direction of travel
which is then reinforced by the fifth of our

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:51.990
new care model vanguards, connecting this
to the previous piece, on acute care collaboration,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:56.910
and addressing the point that the Minister
for Productivity raised recently in a speech

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:03.190
around getting beyond hospitals as ‘islands
of autonomy’.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:11.960
I would finally, Chair, like to commend these
models, standards and specifications to the

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:12.960
Board.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:20.770
I believe that they will help improve patient
care, and they are a further step on the way

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:27.190
to providing resolutions to issues that have
been exhaustively discussed and reviewed over

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:34.710
the last 15 years.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:41.650
I think there is nothing more precious to
any family than their children, and to find

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:48.220
that your children might have a problem with
their heart is quite devastating.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.870
I think children’s heart services are the
quintessential interface, really, between

00:06:53.870 --> 00:07:01.220
science, technology, ethics, fine clinical
judgement and, frankly, in the surgical arena,

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.500
absolutely supreme surgical skill.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:12.740
As a consequence of that, I think we have
in this country very good results for our

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:17.940
endeavour in children’s heart services.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:26.500
However, in July 2001, 14 years ago, Ian Kennedy
submitted a report to Parliament which showed

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:33.120
that there had been some significant failings
in this service in Bristol, and some significant

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.170
lessons which could be learnt for the totality
of the service across England.

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:45.450
Jim Munroe, who was a very well respected
cardiac surgeon in Southampton and subsequently

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:51.280
president of the Society for Cardiothoracic
Surgeons, conducted a second review and came

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:56.160
to the same conclusions on the part of the
Department of Health, but frankly not much

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:57.720
happened.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:03.890
In 2007, when I was president of the Society
of Cardiothoracic Surgeons, I was asked by

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:08.590
colleagues to write to the Department of Health
to point out that children’s heart services

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:14.720
were fragile and that we could not rule out
that there might be another similar set of

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:19.550
problems somewhere; that letter is in the
public domain and well documented.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:28.430
As a consequence of that the NHS Management
Board felt that it was necessary to deal with

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:33.589
some of the existing issues which had been
highlighted, but perhaps more importantly

00:08:33.589 --> 00:08:40.210
to future proof the service for changing science,
technology and clinical practice in quite

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.820
a fast moving technological area.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:50.670
On top of that, it was my view that if you
are going to deal with some problems, then

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:56.480
set your ambition quite high and let us aim
to make our children’s heart services not

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:03.590
just good, but the very best in the world,
and I think that ambition is realisable.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:09.880
The consequence of those discussions led to
a review called the Safe and Sustainable Review,

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:16.020
which was a difficult process which exposed
many shortcomings in many institutions in

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:20.700
the way that the services were delivered,
but the very process of holding up the mirror

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:27.300
to those services ended up in many of the
issues themselves being resolved.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:31.551
In the end, the recommendations which came
out of that review were deemed flawed as a

00:09:31.551 --> 00:09:36.290
consequence of the process, and the Secretary
of State then asked NHS England to pick up

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:43.770
the mantle and see if we could pursue the
ambition to make our services the best in

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:44.990
the world.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:49.320
The end result of that was the New Review,
which has been conducted by Ian’s team,

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:56.270
and in particular, run by John Holden, who
I think deserves a huge amount of credit for

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.640
the amount of work and engagement that he
and his team have put in.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:07.530
What was different about this review was that,
rather than focusing specifically on individual

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:12.610
institutions, it focused on asking those who
deliver the service, who live and breathe

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:18.070
the service on a day-by-day basis – ranging
from cardiologist surgeons through to patients

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:25.000
and parents – what sort of standards should
we set in order to achieve the ambitions that

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:26.000
we want.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:32.730
So this whole process has been based on standards,
and those standards recognise that over the

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:39.070
course of the last few years there has been
massive progress in pre-natal diagnosis of

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:45.460
congenital heart disease; indeed, even in
foetal treatments for congenital heart disease.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:50.110
There have been improvements in surgical and
non surgical treatments for young children,

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:55.460
and as a consequence of that we have seen
quite a significant growth in the number of

00:10:55.460 --> 00:11:01.270
children who live much longer and grow into
adult life, and therefore become adults with

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:05.540
congenital heart disease.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:11.560
That has, as a consequence, led to a focus
on how many of these very different services

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:18.050
can be brought together and helped to function
in a more collaborative way through networks.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:22.860
What is particularly encouraging, in my view,
about all of this is that the standards are

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:29.529
really pretty comprehensive and pretty extensive,
and they have been built by the sorts of people

00:11:29.529 --> 00:11:35.720
that I describe, who agree; there is massive
consensus for over 90% of the standards, something

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:40.190
that we should be really proud of and that
we would not get in many other services.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:46.650
There is inevitably a clutch of standards
where there is still some controversy, and

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:52.770
so I think that will come out in the wash
as different organisations begin to address

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:58.550
them and as the networks come together.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:03.900
My conclusion in all of this is that this
has been a difficult journey which has gone

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:09.190
on, frankly, for over 15 years since the Bristol
enquiry started.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:16.130
We need to get to a point of closure on this,
because it is a small speciality that at times

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:21.220
has ripped itself apart trying to deal with
some of the very difficult issues.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:26.270
People are tired of this, and it is our job,
I think, as a good commissioning organisation

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:31.370
to bring this to a close, to make it much
easier for those who want to practise this

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:36.630
service in an environment which makes it easy
for them to do what is right and proper for

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:42.850
their own patients, and I think we are very
close to that now.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:43.860
Thank you for that, Ian.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:46.320
Thank you, Bruce, for those very thoughtful
remarks.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.480
Just for assurance purposes, to remind the
Board that in addition to the very extensive

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:57.800
documentation we have, there has been from
the outset a Board Task and Finish Group.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:02.540
I have chaired that, I have been joined by
two non-executives, Ed and Margaret, and we

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.820
have tracked the progress of this right through
the last two years.

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:10.710
I would echo obviously what Bruce has said
about the work of John Holden, Mike Wilson

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:18.510
and others in really quite an exemplary open
model of engagement with patients, clinicians

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:23.520
and providers in a way that I do not think
we would have got to this level of consensus

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:30.400
without that, particularly given the damage
of previous reviews, so I am very grateful

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:31.400
to them.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:37.670
I do completely understand that there are
still some difficult issues which I detect

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:43.860
the providers are as keen as we are to resolve,
and if we can do by September/October then

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:49.510
we will be set to have the new system up and
running in time for 2016, which I think is

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:54.940
a fundamental obligation on us if we can possibly
get there.

00:13:54.940 --> 00:14:01.880
Can I just first of all say how I excellent
I think the report is, but there is just one

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:06.760
area where I just wonder whether or not the
ambition could be slightly strengthened, which

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.360
relates to transparency.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.560
One of the great principles which has been
particularly played out largely through Bruce’s

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.750
leadership over the years has been that of
open publication of outcomes, particularly

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:21.390
in relation to surgical disciplines and particularly
in relation to cardiothoracic surgery.

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:27.660
I could just bring the Board’s attention
to paragraph 75; what we are saying here I

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:33.850
think – and I just wanted to clarify – is
that once the data is reliable we will encourage

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:36.290
publication of outcomes.

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:41.100
The word ‘reliable’ just slightly worries
me in the sense that I would hope that, as

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:46.020
we implement this, patients – public advocates
– will be involved in determining at what

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.810
point it is appropriate for this data to be
published.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:52.160
Because for too many years, I am afraid we
have left that responsibility to clinical

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:57.690
colleagues, and we now know that in some cases
that has not been in the patient’s interest.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:02.430
The situation as things stand now, as the
report identifies, is one in which, when there

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:07.242
are concerns from the data about performance,
those concerns are made available to the provider

00:15:07.242 --> 00:15:11.980
but not currently provided to the commissioner,
and not to the patients who are being treated.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:16.430
Therefore, I would just hope that in the context
of so much that is excellent about this report,

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:21.540
we can be very bold and ambitious about putting
the principle of transparency properly to

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.420
work for patients in this context.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.310
Thank you, Tim.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:32.180
First of all, may I say this data has been
in the public domain for a number of years.

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:38.040
It is available on the NICOR website, and
you can drill down to see how each unit is

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:45.540
doing in a vast number of different procedures,
and you can filter that in a number of different

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:46.540
ways.

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:47.652
That is the first point.

00:15:47.652 --> 00:15:53.310
The second point is that there is also a sort
of annual report, if you like, which is not

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:58.480
as timely as it could be because the collection
of some of this data has not been as timely

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:04.399
as it could be, and we have had very vigorous
discussions with NICOR to ensure that we get

00:16:04.399 --> 00:16:09.380
to a place where much more timely data is
collected and then made available.

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:13.839
The third thing is quite an important point.

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:19.399
There is no doubt that when this data is exposed
to parents – and we have seen this most

00:16:19.399 --> 00:16:23.990
recently around Bristol – that parents will
look at it in great detail.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:28.360
Actually, it turns out that they have been
pretty good arbiters of the accuracy of some

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:32.740
of that data.

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:41.420
Bruce, you rightly pointed out that part of
what is going on here is to futureproof the

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:49.279
system, and indeed, to do so by setting high
standards, and to link to Ian’s point that

00:16:49.279 --> 00:16:54.160
in a sense this is a prototype of a series
of rolling service reviews that will be conducted

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.640
across several areas.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:06.199
In this case and in future cases, we are going
to be facing a remodelling of care pathways

00:17:06.199 --> 00:17:11.839
to which many providers are at the moment
some way off the mark.

00:17:11.839 --> 00:17:17.619
What are we going to do to help the providers
to attain the standards, remodel the care

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:22.119
pathways, reach the futureproofing that we
want here?

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:28.249
Because I think our approach to that is not
only crucial here, but will be crucial as

00:17:28.249 --> 00:17:34.249
a role model for how we are going to help
providers generally as each service review

00:17:34.249 --> 00:17:49.269
comes forward, how we help them reach a high
bar which is set.

00:17:49.269 --> 00:17:55.049
This is a relatively small area of clinical
endeavour; although it is a very important

00:17:55.049 --> 00:18:01.499
one in the grand scheme of things, the number
of units, the number of surgeons are all pretty

00:18:01.499 --> 00:18:02.499
small.

00:18:02.499 --> 00:18:07.140
One of the things that we have tried to do
in this current review is to get all the providers

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:12.159
– and that includes managerial colleagues,
clinical colleagues, but providers in the

00:18:12.159 --> 00:18:17.649
broadest sense – into a room to identify
what they think the problems are, and among

00:18:17.649 --> 00:18:22.509
themselves to try and find solutions.

00:18:22.509 --> 00:18:29.179
I think we have a major facilitatory role
in this particular area.

00:18:29.179 --> 00:18:38.029
Just to add: I hope that the design of the
model set out in this review facilitates ongoing

00:18:38.029 --> 00:18:43.149
adaptation and improvement; so, the way in
which networks are established, the way in

00:18:43.149 --> 00:18:50.049
which the networks work with each other, the
way in which information is shared, that peer

00:18:50.049 --> 00:19:02.880
review occurs, and there is mutual assistance
and support, we think will go some way within

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:07.929
what is as Bruce says a relatively small speciality
towards helping with that.

00:19:07.929 --> 00:19:16.539
Supported of course by the myriad professional
organisations; so obviously, not just NHS

00:19:16.539 --> 00:19:22.019
England and the support within Bruce’s medical
directorate, but actually professional bodies

00:19:22.019 --> 00:19:26.210
that are already providing very significant
support across the service.

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:33.110
I think we can be reasonably confident in
this particular area, Noel, that we have a

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:38.850
good answer to that question, perhaps a better
answer to that question than some other specialties.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:48.149
You talk about 16th April, which I think is
right to talk about.

00:19:48.149 --> 00:19:53.480
You talk about the trajectory from here to
get there, and there are twin routes.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:58.690
There is achieving consensus and then there
is the contracting route.

00:19:58.690 --> 00:20:05.450
Have we identified the resources, the timetable
and the processes that are necessary to do

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:11.049
the contracting route now and start that process
now as opposed to later?

00:20:11.049 --> 00:20:18.620
Because you do not get too much of an elapsed
time if you do not start that process.

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:30.700
The review has been developed originally under
Bill McCarthy, then my directorate have continued

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:31.950
that work.

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:37.450
We have been working increasingly closely
with our specialised commissioning colleagues

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:43.320
within commissioning operations under Barbara,
so Richard Jeavons and his team, on developing

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:48.369
the commissioning model that is set out in
this documentation.

00:20:48.369 --> 00:20:56.369
Richard and his team have been developing
that approach, and there is a long handshake

00:20:56.369 --> 00:21:02.279
between the work of my directorate in terms
of developing the standards and then that

00:21:02.279 --> 00:21:09.889
work migrating across to commissioning operations
to then execute and implement.

00:21:09.889 --> 00:21:14.019
I think we are very optimistic that the way
the governance is working, overseen by the

00:21:14.019 --> 00:21:22.690
Task and Finish Group that Malcolm has chaired,
will ensure that that is seamless and that

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:25.940
the preparatory work Richard and the team
have been doing is happening in a timely way

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:29.200
to enable us to go to a procurement option,
should that be necessary.

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:38.720
I think there is a lot of work underway to
prepare, because now it moves on to the hard

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:39.720
bit, which is implementing it.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:43.450
It has been a comprehensive review and very
difficult, but actually they are putting the

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:48.990
final touches to this, and hopefully having
the services in place that we want still requires

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.590
a lot of work.

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:55.509
The bulk of the team is coming over to commissioning
operations, so it is not as if we have a sudden

00:21:55.509 --> 00:21:58.600
loss of that team with expertise.

00:21:58.600 --> 00:22:04.609
The regional directors of specialised commissioning
across the four of our regions will take the

00:22:04.609 --> 00:22:08.809
lead in implementing this, both individually
where there are issues on their patch, and

00:22:08.809 --> 00:22:10.940
collectively where they need to act together.

00:22:10.940 --> 00:22:18.919
We have a group who are meeting already and
who will be looking at submissions with a

00:22:18.919 --> 00:22:24.220
deadline of 9th October, and I think we are
all really optimistic that the submissions

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:30.070
that come in on 9th October will mean that
major procurement is not necessary.

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:34.879
Notwithstanding that, they are absolutely
and entirely aware that they actually have

00:22:34.879 --> 00:22:40.840
to be doing that in parallel, so that if after
9th October there are places in the country

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:42.539
where it is necessary to do some sort of procurement
– because again, there are different models

00:22:42.539 --> 00:22:46.989
for that – what we are doing is working
up the possibilities and the models and the

00:22:46.989 --> 00:22:50.539
way we would procure, should that happen after
9th October.

00:22:50.539 --> 00:22:55.379
I think that, as with everything we do in
NHS England, if the Board is saying, ‘Would

00:22:55.379 --> 00:22:59.369
it be better if we had more resource on this
because it is a massive task?’ then I am

00:22:59.369 --> 00:23:03.710
not going to sit here and say, ‘No, it is
absolutely fine; we have more than enough

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:04.710
people to do this.’

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:08.909
Given the enormity and the breadth of our
agenda, then I think we have a reasonable

00:23:08.909 --> 00:23:10.029
resource at this task.

00:23:10.029 --> 00:23:11.830
We will monitor it closely.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:19.549
I think in NHS England we have become more
adept and more experienced at procurement

00:23:19.549 --> 00:23:22.940
across a range of issues that we have done
recently, and we in Ops will certainly be

00:23:22.940 --> 00:23:27.859
seeking assistance and support, particularly
from Karen in TCO and Paul in his directorate,

00:23:27.859 --> 00:23:30.889
to bring that procurement experience alongside
us.

00:23:30.889 --> 00:23:31.889
Thanks.

00:23:31.889 --> 00:23:34.549
I think I am going to take that as a yes,
then.

00:23:34.549 --> 00:23:41.909
I think the critical thing is: are we geared
up in all contingencies to be able to implement

00:23:41.909 --> 00:23:43.249
this for April next year?

00:23:43.249 --> 00:23:48.739
I really take the final point that Bruce said:
everybody wants certainty.

00:23:48.739 --> 00:23:54.340
Everybody wants to pin this down and to get
on to life creating the highest quality congenital

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:56.529
heart disease services in the world.

00:23:56.529 --> 00:24:02.570
Can I just ask: one obvious issue that we
cannot resolve by next year is the issue of

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:03.570
co-location.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:06.789
It obviously affects my hospital as well as
other hospitals in the country.

00:24:06.789 --> 00:24:13.350
Do we have a feel for a timescale that we
would expect that issue to be addressed, or

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:18.090
are we open to suggestions there?

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:28.480
If I may come in on that, I think there has
been a perception that co-location and the

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:34.409
co-location standards here imply a major reconfiguration
of individual units.

00:24:34.409 --> 00:24:41.379
The purpose of the work of the provider group
has been to explore what different network

00:24:41.379 --> 00:24:49.889
solutions across different units might be
possible in order to develop the service that

00:24:49.889 --> 00:24:52.529
meets the standards.

00:24:52.529 --> 00:24:58.690
It is those conversations that are happening
at the moment and where we will get the responses

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:01.649
back in October, as Barbara has alluded to.

00:25:01.649 --> 00:25:09.009
So I think the extent to which it is or is
not necessary to consider major reconfiguration

00:25:09.009 --> 00:25:11.600
will depend on the provider response.

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:16.570
I think the critical thing for the Board – and
I am going to invite the Board in a moment

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:22.359
to formally agree the questions that are set
out on the front of the paper – is for us

00:25:22.359 --> 00:25:28.049
to be clear that we are aiming for a set of
national standards and specifications.

00:25:28.049 --> 00:25:33.350
We do not want extensive derogations from
those, because that undermines the point of

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:36.279
having done all of this work in the first
place.

00:25:36.279 --> 00:25:40.330
Thirdly, we are anxious in our discussions
with the providers – and very grateful to

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:45.000
Chris Hobson for agreeing to take on this
task to help us – to find through networks

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:51.499
or through other means ways of meeting the
specifications and standards in perhaps novel

00:25:51.499 --> 00:25:55.919
and innovative methods that we have not been
able to advance before, partly because of

00:25:55.919 --> 00:26:00.860
the institutional tensions that some of these
reviews have invoked.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:05.570
Please, if we start from the proposition that
it is our intention to work closely with the

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:10.399
providers to solve the problem, and I know
that the interest in solving the problem is

00:26:10.399 --> 00:26:15.659
widely shared across the provider side as
well, then it is on that basis that I would

00:26:15.659 --> 00:26:18.729
invite the Board to approve the following.

00:26:18.729 --> 00:26:22.950
First, to agree the proposed model of care
and standards and service specifications.

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:25.090
Thank you.

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:29.590
To agree the proposals for earlier diagnosis
and improvements in information, which we

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:33.389
have not touched on but were covered extensively
in the paper.

00:26:33.389 --> 00:26:38.019
We have noted the analysis of the required
service capacity, but I am asking you now

00:26:38.019 --> 00:26:43.710
to agree the proposals for commissioning the
service and implementation, including monitoring

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:46.539
and management, of adherence to the standards.

00:26:46.539 --> 00:26:48.369
The Board has agreed.

00:26:48.369 --> 00:26:49.019
Thank you very much.

